{"schema": 1, "kind": "review", "id": "rev-20261123T230715Z-547f34c4", "ts": "2026-11-23T23:11:35Z", "actor": {"worktree": "prawduct", "plugin": "3.9.1-dev", "branch": "fix/backlog-batch-2026-11-23", "build": "ecd39ffbf786"}, "body": {"base_commit": "882cdafbb896ab3d6fff2fdfc919c5f116a6fdf8", "base_tree": "efd2de1dcf74dbd37e64db2e58a43b7691204471", "head_tree": "a77dd72fab68ecd53d4af8d6b44882e2cdca5049", "head_commit": "ea2268c04b3df8b95dac78b9eb0d44a8828337ec", "dispatch_commit": "ea2268c04b3df8b95dac78b9eb0d44a8828337ec", "mode": "cumulative (bundle review, ready for merge)", "mode_chosen_by": "explicit-args", "stage": "boundary", "tier": "escalate", "roster": [{"role": "correctness", "model": "claude-opus-5-5"}, {"role": "design", "model": "claude-opus-5-5"}, {"role": "sustainability", "model": "claude-opus-5-5"}], "files_reviewed": [".gitignore", "documentation/issues/1222-1223-design.md", "plugin/bin/prawduct-hook", "plugin/lib/backlog/cli.py", "plugin/lib/backlog/query.py", "plugin/lib/backlog/transport.py", "plugin/lib/backlog/upstream.py", "plugin/lib/core.py", "plugin/lib/coverage_algebra.py", "plugin/lib/critic_consolidate.py", "plugin/lib/critic_mode.py", "plugin/skills/backlog/SKILL.md", "plugin/skills/backlog/adapter-mode.md", "tests/fixtures/host_seam_golden/clear_brief_only.json", "tests/fixtures/host_seam_golden/clear_brief_only_config_home.json", "tests/fixtures/host_seam_golden/clear_session_start.json", "tests/test_backlog_pick_history.py", "tests/test_critic_consolidate.py", "tests/test_hook_session_file_registry.py", "tests/test_measure_alignment_yield.py", "tests/test_review_interval_extension.py", "tests/test_verify_links_review_id.py", "tests/test_walk_links_one_owner.py", "tools/measure-alignment-yield.py"], "files_changed": [".gitignore", ".prawduct/artifacts/requirements-alignment-discovery.md", ".prawduct/changes/alignment-yield-windows.md", ".prawduct/changes/pick-log-names-item.md", ".prawduct/changes/recorder-temp-session-file.md", ".prawduct/changes/review-chain-merge-base.md", ".prawduct/project-state.yaml", ".prawduct/research/alignment-yield/baselines.json", "documentation/issues/1222-1223-design.md", "plugin/bin/prawduct-hook", "plugin/lib/backlog/cli.py", "plugin/lib/backlog/query.py", "plugin/lib/backlog/transport.py", "plugin/lib/backlog/upstream.py", "plugin/lib/core.py", "plugin/lib/coverage_algebra.py", "plugin/lib/critic_consolidate.py", "plugin/lib/critic_mode.py", "plugin/skills/backlog/SKILL.md", "plugin/skills/backlog/adapter-mode.md", "tests/fixtures/host_seam_golden/clear_brief_only.json", "tests/fixtures/host_seam_golden/clear_brief_only_config_home.json", "tests/fixtures/host_seam_golden/clear_session_start.json", "tests/test_backlog_pick_history.py", "tests/test_critic_consolidate.py", "tests/test_hook_session_file_registry.py", "tests/test_measure_alignment_yield.py", "tests/test_review_interval_extension.py", "tests/test_verify_links_review_id.py", "tests/test_walk_links_one_owner.py", "tools/measure-alignment-yield.py"], "files_oracle": [".prawduct/artifacts/requirements-alignment-discovery.md", ".prawduct/changes/alignment-yield-windows.md", ".prawduct/changes/pick-log-names-item.md", ".prawduct/changes/recorder-temp-session-file.md", ".prawduct/changes/review-chain-merge-base.md", ".prawduct/project-state.yaml", ".prawduct/research/alignment-yield/baselines.json"], "findings": [{"goal": "Nothing Is Missing", "severity": "warning", "title": "#1202 fixed one atomic-write temp sibling; every other session file written through atomic_write_text leaves an unexempt, unignored `<name>.tmp`", "recommendation": "Why it broke, in one sentence: `core.atomic_write_text` writes every file through `path.name + '.tmp'`, and neither `core.GITIGNORE_ENTRIES` nor `critic_consolidate._is_session_file` knows that a session file's temp sibling is a session file. That sentence covers more than this diff. `git check-ignore` confirms that `.prawduct/.session-handoff.md.tmp`, `.advisories.json.tmp` and `.critic-active.tmp` are not ignored, and `_is_session_file` matches anchored entries by exact path, so none of them is exempt either. Each has both halves of #1202's defect. A tree capture that lands between the write and the rename refuses the review: the main session's Stop writes `.session-handoff.md` while backgrounded reviewers run, and advisory refreshes rewrite `.advisories.json`. A write that dies before the rename leaves an untracked file for the next `git add -A`. Rule cited: 'A review finding names an instance; the defect is a class.' This is a class, and listing more entries will not close it. It closes by construction, in one owner: `_is_session_file` reads `<entry>.tmp` as its entry (or atomic_write_text names its temp file in a form one ignore pattern covers), with a matching managed `.gitignore` pattern. A test should derive the members from GITIGNORE_ENTRIES rather than name them, so `.test-evidence.json.tmp` becomes one member of a rule instead of a one-off entry.", "files": ["plugin/lib/core.py", "plugin/lib/critic_consolidate.py", "plugin/bin/prawduct-hook"], "scope": "class", "closure": "A file under .prawduct/ that is a GITIGNORE_ENTRIES session file and is written through core.atomic_write_text (or another write-then-rename through a `.tmp` sibling), so that `<entry>.tmp` exists between the write and the rename.", "members": [{"id": "m1", "site": ".prawduct/.session-handoff.md (briefing.py atomic_write_text)"}, {"id": "m2", "site": ".prawduct/.advisories.json (advisory_store.write_store)"}, {"id": "m3", "site": ".prawduct/.critic-active (critic_marker atomic_write_text)"}, {"id": "m4", "site": ".prawduct/.critic-findings.json (critic_consolidate atomic_write_text)"}, {"id": "m5", "site": ".prawduct/.session-start, .session-git-baseline, .session-base-tree (prawduct-hook atomic_write_text)"}], "fid": "R-1"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "severity": "warning", "title": "read-cost: pick's base-history check runs one `git log --grep` per candidate, and its bounds cap matches, not the walk, so the accepted undercount buys no bound on the case that costs", "recommendation": "Instance. Why it broke: the walk was shaped around git's whole-message `--grep`, then filtered back to subjects. `git log --max-count=100 --grep=...` stops after 100 MATCHES, so for a candidate that few messages name (the usual case) every call walks the base's whole history. That is up to `_HISTORY_CHECK_CAP` (10) full walks per `pick`, each with a 10s timeout (100s worst case, against `GIT_READ_TIMEOUT_S`'s 'must never hold a pick hostage'). `_HISTORY_GIT_MAX`'s comment and the change record justify the accepted undercount (older subject matches missed behind more than 100 body mentions) with 'an unbounded walk would put the cost on every pick', but the walk is already unbounded wherever matches are scarce. Only subjects count, so a simpler shape exists. Make one `git log --format=%h%x09%s <base>` call in `transport` (bounded by `--max-count`, or by date if a bound is wanted), match every candidate's `_history_pattern` against each subject in Python, and annotate from that one result. That removes the per-candidate subprocess, the 10-candidate cap and its warning, and the body-mention undercount together. Then drop the `_HISTORY_GIT_MAX` rationale and the change record's 'one `git log` per candidate ... a hundred message matches' paragraph.", "files": ["plugin/lib/backlog/query.py", "plugin/lib/backlog/transport.py"], "check": "read-cost", "scope": "instance", "closure": "The per-candidate grep walk in `_annotate_base_history` / `_shas_naming_in_subject`, and the prose that justifies its bounds.", "members": [{"id": "m1", "site": "plugin/lib/backlog/query.py::_shas_naming_in_subject"}, {"id": "m2", "site": "plugin/lib/backlog/query.py::_HISTORY_GIT_MAX comment"}, {"id": "m3", "site": "plugin/lib/backlog/transport.py::git_log_subjects"}], "fid": "R-2"}, {"goal": "The System Can Be Understood", "severity": "warning", "title": "measure-alignment-yield attributes any live-below-saved shortfall to transcript pruning and tells the operator to cite the saved counts, though it records tool_basis and never compares it", "recommendation": "Instance. Why it broke: `_against_saved` decides `comparable` from `min_chars` alone and `pruned` from `live < saved` alone, and its docstring says 'Counts only fall when transcripts are pruned'. That is false whenever the instrument changes, and this same commit lowers `requests`/`substantive`/`corrections` by excluding compaction summaries. `_tool_basis()` is recorded so 'a saved reading names the instrument that produced it', but no comparison reads it, and the human output never prints it. Consequence: after the next edit to the script, a re-run of a saved window that falls short prints 'transcripts were pruned, so cite the saved counts for this window.' The operator then cites a baseline from the old instrument beside `--since` figures from the new one as if they were comparable, which is the mistake the artifact's own 'Figures from runs before that change count compaction summaries' caveat warns about. Fix: compare the saved `tool_basis` with `_tool_basis()`. When they differ, say the instrument changed since the save, so a shortfall may come from the instrument and not from pruning, and print the saved basis. Add a test with a saved entry whose tool_basis differs from the live one. Correct the docstring sentence in the same change.", "files": ["tools/measure-alignment-yield.py", "tests/test_measure_alignment_yield.py"], "scope": "instance", "closure": "The baseline comparison's pruned and comparable verdicts in tools/measure-alignment-yield.py `_against_saved` and the human output that prints them.", "members": [{"id": "m1", "site": "tools/measure-alignment-yield.py::_against_saved"}], "fid": "R-3"}], "counts": {"blocking": 0, "warning": 3, "note": 0}, "observations": [{"goal": "Nothing Is Unintended", "title": "resolve_base_ref's docstring says pick and the gates 'cannot name different bases', but they can", "recommendation": "`transport.resolve_base_ref` calls `coverage.repo_base_branch`. The gates resolve through `coverage._resolve_base_branch`, which puts a bound plan's `base:` first, so on a stacked unit pick and the gates do read different refs. For pick, the repo's integration base is arguably the right ref, so delete the 'cannot name different bases' clause rather than change the code. A second case: when only the `HEAD~1` candidate resolves, the history note reports this branch's own commits as 'on HEAD~1'. `chain_bound` refuses that moving fallback and pick does not. The note is advice only, so nothing is gated on it.", "files": ["plugin/lib/backlog/transport.py", "plugin/lib/backlog/query.py"], "oid": "O-1"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Broken", "title": "The saved-baseline comparison blames any shortfall on pruning, even though it records the instrument (tool_basis) and never compares it", "recommendation": "`_against_saved` sets `pruned` whenever a live count is below its saved count, and the docstring says 'counts only fall when transcripts are pruned'. A tool change that lowers counts, like #1211's own compaction exclusion, would print 'transcripts were pruned'. `comparable` checks only `min_chars`. The seeded 2026-10-09 window is not affected, because the change record says it held no compaction summaries. A future re-read after another instrument change would misattribute the drop.", "files": ["tools/measure-alignment-yield.py"], "oid": "O-2"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Unintended", "title": "Governing-Artifact Reconciliation: nothing to reconcile", "recommendation": "No plan claims this branch (the manifest scope is unbound), and none of the four change records or the design doc carries a `governed_by:` disposition, so no recorded disposition could be checked against the diff.", "oid": "O-3"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "title": "chain_bound resolves the merge base twice: once itself, once inside `_branch_commits`", "recommendation": "`chain_bound` calls `coverage.resolve_merge_base_tree` (three git calls) for the tree and `moving`. It then calls `evidence_notes._branch_commits`, which resolves the same merge base again before its `rev-list`, so the bound's tree and commits come from two separate resolutions. The Stop path's `_newest_branch_review` \u2192 `settled_branch_review` runs `_branch_commits` a third time. One fix: have `_branch_commits` return the `tree` and `moving` it already holds in `base`, and build `ChainBound` from that one call. The cost is small, which is why this is an observation.", "files": ["plugin/lib/critic_mode.py"], "oid": "O-4"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "title": "Every production caller writes `review_chain(facts, x, bound=chain_bound(project_dir))`; the pairing has no owner", "recommendation": "All four production sites compose the same pair. Only tests pass `bound=None`. The required keyword makes a caller think about it but does not stop a future caller from passing `None` or a different bound. The per-site test pins only the four callers known today. A `branch_review_chain(project_dir, facts, newest)` wrapper would be the one owner (core.md: 'fix the class through one owner'), with `review_chain(..., bound=)` kept for tests. Optional.", "files": ["plugin/lib/critic_mode.py", "plugin/lib/critic_consolidate.py"], "oid": "O-5"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "title": "New cross-module use of private helpers: `coverage_algebra._walk_identity`, `evidence_notes._branch_commits`", "recommendation": "`critic_mode.review_chain` now filters on `coverage_algebra._walk_identity`, and `chain_bound` calls `evidence_notes._branch_commits`. The latter already has three cross-module callers and calls itself 'the bound every bounded read and repair shares', so it is public in all but name. If these are shared seams, drop the underscore. Inert today.", "files": ["plugin/lib/critic_mode.py"], "oid": "O-6"}, {"goal": "Everything Is Coherent", "title": "`resolve_base_ref` says pick and the gates 'cannot name different bases', but it delegates to `repo_base_branch`, while the gates resolve through `_resolve_base_branch`, which prefers a stacked unit's plan `base:`", "recommendation": "On a stacked unit (a plan declaring `base: feature/A`), `pick` checks the repo-level base, which is arguably the right base for 'has it shipped'. The gates, via `resolve_merge_base_tree` \u2192 `_resolve_base_branch`, use `feature/A`. The docstring and the change record ('the same resolver the gates use') overstate this. Make the claim relational: say it is the repo-level resolver and a plan's `base:` is not consulted. Prose only; nothing reads it.", "files": ["plugin/lib/backlog/transport.py"], "oid": "O-7"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "title": "`measure()` returns results through two out-parameters (`health`, `selected`)", "recommendation": "`selected` joins `health` as a list the caller passes in to be filled. Returning a small result (by, health, selected) would be more idiomatic. This is a research tool with one caller, so no change is needed.", "files": ["tools/measure-alignment-yield.py"], "oid": "O-8"}, {"goal": "The System Can Be Understood", "title": "pick's history annotation cannot be told apart from a check that never ran: a skipped tracker (scope is not origin, or no readable origin) and 'checked, nothing matched' give the same output", "recommendation": "This is deliberate and documented ('Neither is an error, so neither speaks'; adapter-mode.md and the consumer note say it appears only when --repo is the checkout's origin). If a consumer later reports confusion, one `history_checked` boolean in the JSON result would separate the two states without adding noise to human output. No edit is wanted now.", "files": ["plugin/lib/backlog/query.py"], "oid": "O-9"}, {"goal": "The System Can Be Understood", "title": "When chain_bound is None the review chain walks unbounded and leaves no trace that it did", "recommendation": "The fallback and its reason are recorded with the rejected alternative (review_chain docstring and the review-chain-merge-base change record), so the decision was deliberate. The leak it re-admits needs a resolvable merge base, so the silent state should be rare. No edit is wanted; this is noted so a future 'why did my boundary read covered' investigation knows the unbounded state exists.", "files": ["plugin/lib/critic_mode.py"], "oid": "O-10"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Broken", "title": "Outside my goals, flagged for the coordinator: #1202 exempted one atomic-write temp sibling, but core.atomic_write_text writes '<name>.tmp' for other .prawduct/ state files", "recommendation": "Core rule 'A review finding names an instance; the defect is a class.' tree_changed_since_dispatch exempts only exact GITIGNORE_ENTRIES paths. The writer at plugin/lib/core.py:274 (`path.with_name(path.name + '.tmp')`), host.py:583 (`{cache_path}.{pid}.tmp`) and learnings_compact.py:543 make transient siblings that the one added entry does not cover. Whether any of them lands under .prawduct/ during a review is for the correctness reviewer (Goals 1 and 3) to rate. A glob entry or a temp-name convention owned by atomic_write_text would close the class through one owner.", "files": ["plugin/lib/core.py", "plugin/lib/critic_consolidate.py"], "oid": "O-11"}], "duration_seconds": 420, "scope": null, "scope_chosen_by": "none", "scope_unresolved_cause": "unbound", "scope_reason": "no live plan claims `fix/backlog-batch-2026-11-23`", "base_extended_from": null, "chunks": [], "chunks_proposed": [], "chunks_declared": false, "chunks_evidence": {}, "base_reviewed": "882cdafbb896ab3d6fff2fdfc919c5f116a6fdf8", "record_lint": {"records": [".prawduct/artifacts/requirements-alignment-discovery.md", ".prawduct/changes/alignment-yield-windows.md", ".prawduct/changes/pick-log-names-item.md", ".prawduct/changes/recorder-temp-session-file.md", ".prawduct/changes/review-chain-merge-base.md", ".prawduct/pro